-- Q1: easy / L1 — Compilation engagement assurance level
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$What level of assurance does a CPA provide in a compilation engagement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No assurance", "Limited assurance", "Reasonable assurance", "Absolute assurance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AR-C 80.05 states that in a compilation engagement, the accountant does not express an opinion or provide any assurance on the financial statements. The CPA assists management in presenting financial information without undertaking to obtain or provide any assurance that there are no material modifications needed.
Wrong (B): Limited assurance is provided in a review engagement, not a compilation.
Wrong (C): Reasonable assurance is the level provided in an audit engagement.
Wrong (D): Absolute assurance is never provided in any type of engagement due to inherent limitations.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Preconditions for audit when management refuses responsibility
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Reeves & Associates, CPAs, is discussing engagement terms with a prospective client. During negotiations, management states that it does not believe it has responsibility for the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements and refuses to include this acknowledgment in the engagement letter. How should the firm respond?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept the engagement but document the disagreement in the working papers", "Decline to accept the audit engagement", "Accept the engagement and modify the audit report to note the disagreement", "Accept the engagement under a special purpose framework instead"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 210.06 requires that the auditor establish whether the preconditions for an audit are present before accepting an engagement. A key precondition is management's acknowledgment of its responsibility for preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements. If management refuses this acknowledgment, the preconditions are not met and the auditor must not accept the engagement.
Wrong (A): Documenting the disagreement does not cure the absence of a required precondition.
Wrong (C): Modifying the report is not a remedy when preconditions for the engagement itself are absent.
Wrong (D): Switching frameworks does not eliminate the requirement for management to accept its responsibilities.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Agreed-upon procedures engagement characteristics
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Greenfield Hospital engages Martin, CPA, to perform agreed-upon procedures on its patient billing system. Martin applies only the specific procedures that Greenfield and its insurer have agreed to. Which statement best describes the resulting report?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Martin expresses an opinion on the overall billing system", "Martin provides limited assurance that nothing came to his attention", "Martin reports findings without providing assurance or an opinion", "Martin provides reasonable assurance that the billing system is free of errors"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AT-C 215.04 states that in an agreed-upon procedures engagement, the practitioner reports findings based on the procedures performed without providing an opinion or any form of assurance. The specified parties draw their own conclusions from the findings reported.
Wrong (A): An opinion is expressed in an examination or audit, not in agreed-upon procedures.
Wrong (B): Limited assurance is the hallmark of a review engagement, not agreed-upon procedures.
Wrong (D): Reasonable assurance relates to audit engagements, not agreed-upon procedures.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Professional skepticism definition
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$What does professional skepticism require of an auditor?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Assuming management is dishonest until proven otherwise", "Accepting all management representations without question", "Relying exclusively on external third-party confirmations", "Maintaining a questioning mind and critically assessing audit evidence"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): AU-C 200.14 defines professional skepticism as an attitude that includes a questioning mind, being alert to conditions that may indicate possible misstatement, and a critical assessment of audit evidence. It does not presume dishonesty but also does not assume unquestioned honesty.
Wrong (A): Professional skepticism does not presume dishonesty; it maintains a neutral questioning attitude.
Wrong (B): Accepting representations without question is the opposite of professional skepticism.
Wrong (C): Relying exclusively on external confirmations ignores other valid forms of evidence and does not define skepticism.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Scope limitation discovered mid-audit and engagement considerations
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$During an audit of Warner Industries, Chen, CPA, discovers that the client's warehouse suffered a flood destroying all physical inventory records for six months of the year. The inventory account represents 40% of total assets. Chen has exhausted all alternative procedures and cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence regarding inventory. Which course of action is most appropriate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Withdraw from the engagement because sufficient evidence cannot be obtained for a material account", "Issue an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph", "Issue a qualified or disclaimer of opinion depending on the pervasiveness of the limitation", "Issue an adverse opinion due to the material misstatement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 705.13 requires the auditor to disclaim an opinion when unable to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence and the possible effects are both material and pervasive. With inventory at 40% of total assets and no alternative procedures available, the effect is pervasive. AU-C 705.A13 notes that when the scope limitation is imposed by circumstances and is pervasive, withdrawal may be the appropriate response, particularly in initial engagements.
Wrong (B): An unmodified opinion is inappropriate when sufficient evidence cannot be obtained for a material account.
Wrong (C): While a qualified opinion addresses material-but-not-pervasive limitations, 40% of total assets is pervasive, making disclaimer or withdrawal appropriate.
Wrong (D): An adverse opinion addresses material misstatements identified, not an inability to obtain evidence.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — PCAOB vs AICPA standard applicability
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Collins Manufacturing is a publicly traded company listed on the NYSE. Its subsidiary, Collins Private Services, is a nonissuer with separate financial statements audited by a different firm. Which standards govern each audit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["PCAOB standards for both entities", "PCAOB standards for Collins Manufacturing; AICPA GAAS for Collins Private Services", "AICPA GAAS for both entities", "AICPA GAAS for Collins Manufacturing; PCAOB standards for Collins Private Services"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under SOX Section 103, the PCAOB establishes auditing standards for audits of issuers (public companies). The AICPA Auditing Standards Board issues Statements on Auditing Standards (SAS) that govern audits of nonissuers. Collins Manufacturing, as an issuer, falls under PCAOB standards, while the separate nonissuer subsidiary audit follows AICPA GAAS.
Wrong (A): PCAOB standards apply only to issuers, not to standalone audits of nonissuers.
Wrong (C): AICPA GAAS does not govern audits of issuers; those fall under PCAOB authority.
Wrong (D): This reverses the correct application of standards to each entity type.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Negative assurance in review engagements
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$What form of assurance does a review engagement report express?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Positive assurance that financial statements are fairly stated", "A disclaimer stating no opinion is expressed", "Negative assurance that nothing came to the accountant's attention requiring material modification", "Findings based on specified procedures without conclusions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AR-C 90.39 requires the accountant's review report to state that the accountant is not aware of any material modifications that should be made to the financial statements. This negative form of assurance indicates limited, rather than reasonable, assurance.
Wrong (A): Positive assurance is expressed in an audit engagement, not a review.
Wrong (B): A disclaimer with no opinion is characteristic of a compilation, not a review.
Wrong (D): Reporting findings without conclusions describes an agreed-upon procedures engagement.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Applying the audit risk model
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$During planning for the audit of Pinnacle Corp, the engagement partner assesses inherent risk as high and control risk as high for the revenue account due to a complex new sales arrangement. To maintain audit risk at an acceptably low level, which adjustment to the planned audit approach is most appropriate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Reduce the extent of substantive procedures for revenue", "Accept a higher level of detection risk for the revenue account", "Reduce detection risk by increasing the nature, timing, and extent of substantive procedures", "Issue a qualified opinion on the revenue account before completing fieldwork"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 200.A42 describes the audit risk model: AR = IR × CR × DR. When inherent risk and control risk are both assessed as high, the auditor must set detection risk low to keep overall audit risk acceptably low. This requires more persuasive substantive procedures — increased extent, more effective nature, and testing closer to year-end.
Wrong (A): Reducing substantive procedures would increase detection risk, raising overall audit risk above acceptable levels.
Wrong (B): Accepting higher detection risk when IR and CR are high would result in unacceptably high audit risk.
Wrong (D): Issuing a qualified opinion before completing fieldwork is premature and not a response to assessed risk.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Engagement letter required content
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Harper, CPA, is drafting an engagement letter for a new audit client, Dalton Enterprises. Dalton's controller asks whether the letter must address management's responsibilities. Which element is required in the audit engagement letter under auditing standards?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Management's acknowledgment of responsibility for the financial statements and internal control", "A guarantee that all fraud will be detected during the audit", "The specific audit procedures the auditor plans to perform", "The projected timeline for completing each phase of the audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 210.10 requires the engagement letter to include, among other items, management's acknowledgment of its responsibility for the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements in accordance with the applicable framework, and for internal control necessary to prepare financial statements free from material misstatement.
Wrong (B): No engagement letter can guarantee fraud detection; audits provide reasonable, not absolute, assurance.
Wrong (C): Specific procedures are determined during planning and fieldwork, not prescribed in the engagement letter.
Wrong (D): A projected timeline is a practical matter but not a required element under AU-C 210.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L3 — Special purpose framework identification
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Ortega, CPA, is engaged to audit the financial statements of a local government utility that prepares its statements on the regulatory basis prescribed by the state public utilities commission rather than GAAP. The regulatory basis includes recognition and measurement principles that differ significantly from GAAP. How should Ortega classify this financial reporting framework?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["General purpose framework requiring an unmodified opinion", "Special purpose framework — regulatory basis", "Special purpose framework — contractual basis", "Noncompliant framework requiring an adverse opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 800.07 identifies a regulatory basis of accounting as one of four types of special purpose frameworks (cash, tax, regulatory, contractual). When an entity prepares financial statements using a basis prescribed by a regulatory body, the framework is classified as a regulatory basis special purpose framework, and the auditor follows AU-C 800 reporting requirements.
Wrong (A): A regulatory basis is not a general purpose framework; it is designed for a specific regulatory purpose.
Wrong (C): A contractual basis is prescribed by a contract, not a government regulatory agency.
Wrong (D): Using a regulatory basis does not render the framework noncompliant; it is an acceptable special purpose framework.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Preconditions — acceptable financial reporting framework
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Before accepting an audit engagement, Torres, CPA, evaluates whether the financial reporting framework to be applied in preparing the client's financial statements is acceptable. The client proposes using a framework that omits the statement of cash flows and several required disclosures. What is the primary concern under auditing standards?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The framework may not be acceptable because it does not result in adequate disclosures for intended users", "The auditor should accept the engagement and issue a qualified opinion for the omissions", "The auditor must require the client to switch to GAAP before accepting", "The framework is acceptable as long as the client's industry commonly uses it"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 210.06(a) establishes that a precondition for an audit is determining whether the financial reporting framework to be applied is acceptable. AU-C 210.A5 notes that a framework is generally acceptable when it results in financial statements that provide adequate information for intended users. Significant omissions of required statements and disclosures raise concerns about framework acceptability.
Wrong (B): Accepting the engagement with plans to qualify is not appropriate when the precondition of an acceptable framework has not been established.
Wrong (C): While GAAP is preferable, the standard does not mandate GAAP; the framework must be acceptable, not necessarily GAAP.
Wrong (D): Industry commonality alone does not make a framework acceptable if it fails to provide adequate information.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — GAGAS additional scope requirements
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Perry, CPA, is engaged to audit a nonprofit organization that receives significant federal funding. The engagement must comply with Government Auditing Standards (Yellow Book). Compared to a standard GAAS audit, which additional scope requirement does Perry face?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Perry must assess the entity's compliance with tax filing deadlines", "Perry must limit testing to only the federally funded programs", "Perry must report on internal control and compliance with applicable laws and regulations", "Perry may reduce substantive procedures because government entities have stronger internal controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): GAGAS 6.11–6.40 expand audit scope beyond GAAS by requiring the auditor to report on internal control over financial reporting and on compliance with provisions of laws, regulations, contracts, and grant agreements that could have a material effect on the financial statements. These additional reporting requirements are a key distinction of Yellow Book audits.
Wrong (A): Assessing tax filing deadlines is not a specific additional requirement of GAGAS beyond normal GAAS.
Wrong (B): GAGAS requires auditing all financial statements, not limiting scope to only federally funded programs.
Wrong (D): Government entities do not necessarily have stronger controls, and GAGAS does not permit reducing substantive procedures on that assumption.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Fair presentation vs compliance framework
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$During engagement planning, Nelson, CPA, determines that the client prepares financial statements under a compliance framework rather than a fair presentation framework. Which characteristic distinguishes a compliance framework from a fair presentation framework?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A compliance framework requires the entity to provide disclosures beyond those specifically required by the framework", "A compliance framework requires only compliance with the framework's requirements without an obligation for additional disclosures to achieve fair presentation", "A compliance framework applies only to governmental entities", "A compliance framework prohibits the auditor from expressing an opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 200.14 distinguishes between fair presentation and compliance frameworks. A compliance framework requires only compliance with the requirements of the framework; it does not require additional disclosures beyond what the framework specifically mandates. In contrast, a fair presentation framework may require disclosures beyond those specifically required to achieve fair presentation.
Wrong (A): Requiring disclosures beyond those specifically required is a characteristic of a fair presentation framework, not a compliance framework.
Wrong (C): Compliance frameworks are not limited to governmental entities; tax-basis and regulatory-basis frameworks are compliance frameworks used across sectors.
Wrong (D): The auditor can and does express an opinion on compliance framework financial statements.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Predecessor auditor communication in initial engagement
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Liu, CPA, has been approached by Westbrook Inc. to perform its first audit. The company was previously audited by another firm. Before accepting the engagement, Liu plans to communicate with the predecessor auditor. What is the primary purpose of this communication?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To obtain the predecessor's working papers as a substitute for performing audit procedures", "To gather information that may bear on the acceptance decision, including disagreements with management and reasons for the change in auditors", "To obtain the predecessor auditor's written consent to accept the engagement", "To transfer responsibility for the prior-year audit opinion to the successor auditor"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 210.11 requires the auditor, as part of establishing whether preconditions for an audit are present, to request management's authorization to communicate with the predecessor auditor. AU-C 210.A22 indicates this communication helps the successor auditor learn about matters such as integrity of management, disagreements, and reasons for the change, which bear on the engagement acceptance decision.
Wrong (A): The predecessor's working papers supplement but do not substitute for the successor's own audit procedures.
Wrong (C): The predecessor does not grant or withhold consent for the successor to accept the engagement; client permission is needed to facilitate communication.
Wrong (D): Responsibility for the prior-year opinion remains with the predecessor auditor and does not transfer.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Recurring engagement changed circumstances
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Becker, CPA, has audited Maxwell Corp for three years under an ongoing engagement letter. In the current year, Maxwell undergoes a significant restructuring, adopts a new financial reporting framework, and replaces its entire senior management team. Becker reviews the existing engagement terms. Under AU-C 210, what is Becker's obligation regarding the engagement letter?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No action is required because the original engagement letter covers recurring engagements", "Becker should send a reminder letter referencing the original terms", "Becker should assess whether circumstances require revised engagement terms and establish a new or revised engagement letter", "Becker must withdraw from the engagement and require Maxwell to seek a new auditor"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 210.13 states that in recurring audits, the auditor should assess whether circumstances require the terms to be revised and whether there is a need to remind the entity of the existing terms. AU-C 210.A27 identifies changes in senior management, significant changes in the nature or size of the entity, and changes in the financial reporting framework as factors that may necessitate a new engagement letter with revised terms.
Wrong (A): The original letter does not automatically cover changed circumstances; the auditor must evaluate whether revised terms are needed.
Wrong (B): A simple reminder is insufficient when the fundamental nature of the engagement circumstances has changed.
Wrong (D): Changed circumstances do not require withdrawal; they require reassessment and potential revision of the engagement terms.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Attestation engagement definition
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$What type of engagement involves a CPA examining, reviewing, or performing agreed-upon procedures on subject matter other than financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Compilation engagement", "Consulting engagement", "Tax preparation engagement", "Attestation engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): AT-C 105.01 defines attestation engagements as those in which a CPA is engaged to issue an examination, review, or agreed-upon procedures report on subject matter or an assertion that is the responsibility of another party. These engagements cover subject matter beyond historical financial statements, such as internal controls, compliance, or prospective information.
Wrong (A): A compilation involves assisting in preparing financial statements, not examining other subject matter.
Wrong (B): A consulting engagement provides advice and recommendations but does not result in an attestation report.
Wrong (C): Tax preparation is a separate service that does not constitute an attestation engagement.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Quality management standards and engagement scope
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$Fieldstone & Associates recently adopted a system of quality management in accordance with professional standards. During engagement acceptance for a new client, the firm's quality policies require an assessment of whether the firm has the competence, capabilities, and resources to perform the engagement. Which standard establishes this firm-level quality management requirement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Statement on Quality Management Standards No. 1 (SQMS 1)", "AU-C Section 220, Quality Management for an Engagement", "Statement on Auditing Standards No. 145", "AT-C Section 105, Concepts Common to All Attestation Engagements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): SQMS 1.30 requires firms to establish policies and procedures addressing engagement acceptance and continuance, including evaluating whether the firm has the competence, capabilities, and resources to perform the engagement. This is a firm-level quality management requirement distinct from engagement-level quality management.
Wrong (B): AU-C 220 addresses quality management at the individual engagement level, not the firm-level system of quality management.
Wrong (C): SAS 145 addresses understanding the entity and assessing risks, not firm quality management.
Wrong (D): AT-C 105 addresses concepts for attestation engagements, not firm-level quality management systems.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Audit risk definition
(1, 'Nature and Scope of Engagements', $EXPL$What is audit risk?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The risk that the client will go bankrupt during the audit", "The risk that the auditor expresses an inappropriate opinion when the financial statements are materially misstated", "The risk that the client fails to pay the audit fee", "The risk that the engagement team lacks sufficient training"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 200.14 defines audit risk as the risk that the auditor expresses an inappropriate audit opinion when the financial statements are materially misstated. Audit risk is a function of the risks of material misstatement (inherent and control risk) and detection risk.
Wrong (A): Client bankruptcy is a business risk, not the definition of audit risk under auditing standards.
Wrong (C): Fee collection is a business matter for the firm, not a component of audit risk.
Wrong (D): Training adequacy relates to engagement quality, not the technical definition of audit risk.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1)
;

